//! Concrete `PeerResolver` implementation — mesh-aware, health-filtered.
//!
//! This is the bottom-up foundation: without a concrete resolver, every layer
//! above (autonomy loop, CLI, MCP, takeover decision) cannot be tested.

## 15. Fleet and Federation

Status: `partial`

A CYNIC node is a sovereign recursion root. A fleet coordinates nodes that share
an operational authority. Federation connects independently administered trust
or authority domains.

Fleet concerns:

- placement;
- rollout;
- node health;
- leases and fencing;
- artifact transport;
- aggregate budgets;
- rollback and recovery.

Federation adds:

- explicit trust establishment;
- foreign authority references;
- identity and proof verification;
- policy negotiation;
- revocation and key rotation;
- conflict and partial-connectivity semantics.

Scale changes data and adapters, not the core reconciliation law.

//! Federation runtime wiring — composes the four autonomy models via injectable ports.
//!
//! The pure decision lives in `cynic_node_protocol::federation::reconcile_federation`.
//! This module wires it to impure observation sources (kernel reachability,
//! mesh peers, OS health, lease status). Every port is injectable so the
//! composition is unit-testable without real infrastructure.

//! Architecture boundary — cynic-node-protocol.
//!
//! Protocol crate: Tier 2 (Node Domain Protocols).
//! Depends only on cynic-protocol primitives.
//! Never on host, node, kernel, cli, mcp, or any orchestrator crate.

//! Port: mesh **membership** coordination — who may join, who is revoked.
//!
//! This is deliberately separate from `MeshAdapter` (packet path, interfaces,
//! routes) and from `ProviderControlPort` (machine power on a hypervisor).
//! Carrying traffic, powering a machine, and deciding membership are three
//! different authorities; collapsing them is how a coordination provider ends
//! up treated as network truth.
//!
//! It is also distinct from `cynic_node_protocol::machine_enrollment::MachineEnrollment`,
//! which is the signed identity of a CYNIC *node*. A node can be enrolled in
//! CYNIC and absent from the mesh, or present on the mesh without a CYNIC
//! identity. Membership here means only: may this key participate in the
//! overlay.
//!
//! Sovereignty is enforced one layer up by
//! `cynic_network::admit_mesh_provider`: an implementor whose control
//! plane is SaaS is refused before it reaches this port, so nothing here needs
//! to re-check it. Today the only admitted implementor is self-hosted Headscale
//! (LXC 100, `10.10.10.40:8080` — `/health` 200, `/api/v1/node` 401, i.e. alive
//! and authenticating).

/// Policy governing node pre-authentication admission into the WireGuard mesh.
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]
pub struct MeshAdmissionPolicy {
    /// Mandated user namespace (usually "cynic").
    pub mandated_user: String,
    /// Whether ephemeral keys are required for worker nodes.
    pub require_ephemeral: bool,
    /// Mandated ACL tag prefix (e.g. "tag:agent" or "tag:node").
    pub mandated_tag_prefix: String,
}

//! Typed Federation Envelopes, Leases, and Mesh Probe Schemas.
//!
//! Provides the immutable contracts for distributing tasks across sovereign CYNIC nodes
//! without global single-point-of-failure or authority amplification.

//! Node-owned coordination posture (design D: the kernel is a federation
//! peer, never a coordination SPOF).
//!
//! This use case is the first consumer of the previously quarantined
//! `coordination_authority` contract: it reconciles a node's local
//! coordination authority (desired membership/quorum/term/fencing vs observed
//! members) into an executable plan and classifies the resulting posture.
//!
//! Authority stays node-local by construction:
//! - quorum/fencing decisions are computed from local desired state plus
//!   locally observed member health — no kernel round-trip;
//! - mutation fencing is delegated to the node's own lease store
//!   (`StorageLeasePort` adapters) so fencing tokens stay monotonic without
//!   any central issuer;
//! - kernel unavailability can only *degrade* the posture to explicit local
//!   autonomy; it never blocks observation or reconciliation.

